data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Outlining Further Requirements**\n\nI'm now integrating detailed structural components. I must include the closest prior work (authors, year, title), specifying if from memory, followed by a falsifiable experiment, feasible on an 8B model. My contribution type must be clearly defined, with details on what renders the question uninteresting. I also have to explain how I'll verify any claimed novelty. Currently, I am drafting piece 10, considering three papers.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 1709,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 640,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Unifying the Challenges**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a unifying concept that ties together collaborative sabotage, attacks on monitors, and long-term subversion. The common thread I'm seeing is how adversaries exploit shared representation spaces and limited verification budgets. This boils down to \"budgeted sequential/distributed hypothesis testing under adversarial signal dilution,\" or, alternatively, \"optimal audit allocation.\" I'm aiming to articulate this as a consistent framework.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 1921,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 852,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing the Dissimilarities**\n\nI'm now considering the potential orthogonality of the three challenges. It appears temporal dilution, multi-agent collusion, and monitor evasion may operate under different mathematical principles. My current focus is to form a crisp, falsifiable thesis, recognizing when these aspects should remain distinct. I have formulated a question and an expected, falsifiable answer concerning optimal budget allocation. Specifically, I'm examining if budget allocation obeys a common law of trade-off under temporal degradation, multi-agent attacks, or evasion of representation space.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 2124,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1055,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining the Experiment**\n\nI'm now refining the experiment details for piece 10, focusing on comparing a unified budget allocation strategy against three specialized strategies within the context of subtle bias introduction across code synthesis or document analysis. Specifically, I'm examining Greenblatt et al. (2024), cited from memory, in the AI control field and adapting the experiment using Llama-3-8B-Instruct on a simplified SWE-bench Lite or HotpotQA multi-step setup. The goal is to generate subtle biases across 50 sequential steps, using three agents (coder, tester, reviewer), minimizing a linear monitor's score. I'm allotting a fixed budget for audits and re-executions to test whether the unified approach (based on representation divergence and monitor uncertainty) improves the detection. I expect that a common law of trade-off exists between the three different cases.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 2435,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1366,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Contribution**\n\nI'm currently articulating my contribution. It's an application of distributed sequential detection theory/contextual bandits, with adversarial corruption and Wald's likelihood ratio tests, to the control of agents.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI've specified conditions under which the contribution would be uninteresting: if a linear probe detects malicious intent with high accuracy from the first step without comparative executions or audits, or if subtle degradation fails on an 8B model. Novelty will be verified through a literature search on Google Scholar and Semantic Scholar, cross-referencing \"AI control,\" \"activation monitoring,\" \"sequential hypothesis testing,\" and \"multi-agent collusion\" to confirm that no unified budget allocation function for internal probes and counterfactual re-executions has been published. I am now drafting piece 11.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 2667,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1598,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Task Length**\n\nI'm now analyzing the impact of task length on both adversary and defender strategies. As the task length increases, the adversary can dilute malicious signals, and, to steer a trajectory, must maintain a persistent latent intent. The defender's text monitoring degrades, but cumulative activation monitoring and comparison runs become more valuable. I am exploring this trade off.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 2845,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1776,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Task Dynamics**\n\nI'm now formulating a crisp question and falsifiable answer regarding task length. I'm exploring how increasing task length impacts both adversary and defender strategies. My current hypothesis is that while step-by-step text monitoring degrades, longitudinal activation monitoring combined with comparison runs becomes more effective at detecting persistent adversarial intent. Specifically, I expect that a cumulative filter on activations, coupled with comparison executions, maintains a growing detection rate as task length increases, distinguishing intentional drift from random errors.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 3136,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2067,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Formulating Experimental Details**\n\nI'm now detailing the experimental setup for piece 11. I am building off of previous work by MacDiarmid et al. (2024), cited from memory, and Greenblatt et al. (2024), also cited from memory. I will use Llama-3-8B-Instruct to run data analysis or document research tasks of variable length ($T$). I expect my experimental answer will be refuted if AUROC at the trajectory level decreases or stagnates as task length increases. I'm focusing on defining the agents (benign, sabotage) and the variable tasks.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 3456,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2387,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Detection Methods**\n\nI'm now refining detection methods for subtle sabotage with Llama-3-8B-Instruct. I'm focusing on cumulative sum (CUSUM) and comparison executions to measure directional divergence. I'll train a linear probe on residual activations at each step. This work builds on prior work by MacDiarmid et al. (2024), cited from memory, in document analysis with CUSUM for sequential anomaly detection.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 3691,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2622,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Agent Homogeneity**\n\nI'm now focusing on how the fact that agents are multiple copies of the same model alters adversary and defender strategies. Adversaries can leverage shared inductive biases for tacit collusion, which could include the sharing of steganographic triggers. For the defender, this could potentially offer new avenues for detection based on shared representation anomalies. I'm exploring prior work from (Zou et al. 2023) and in the field of AI control.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 3925,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2856,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Homogeneity Impact**\n\nNow, I'm analyzing how identical agents influence the strategies of both attackers and defenders. The symmetry of activations and counterfactual substitutability provide the defender an exceptional baseline. Because of identical weights, the defender may run the same representation probe, or compare activations. This homogeneity creates a measurable cross-agent activation covariance around hidden defects and offers new avenues for detection.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 4154,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3085,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Covariance Advantage**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the defender's advantage when agents are identical, as opposed to the attacker's shared blind spots. I'm focusing on evaluating the cross-agent activation covariance around hidden defects and how this is measurably stronger than in a shared honest error situation. I'm building a falsifiable hypothesis around how the defender can leverage this homogeneity. My expectation is the cross-agent activation covariance will differentiate hidden defects far better than the blind spots of textual monitors.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1069,"totalTokenCount": 4400,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1069}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3331,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "zPwCa4iCMtyh5NoPpPm4mAc"}

